# Newton's Laws of Motion: Force, Momentum, and Friction

> Three short laws explain why things start, stop, and turn. This
> lesson covers inertia, F = ma, action–reaction pairs, momentum,
> its conservation, friction, and circular motion.

## 1. Force and the First Law of Motion

A **force** is a push or pull on a body. Aristotle thought motion
needs a continuous push; Galileo's inclined planes said otherwise,
and Newton sharpened the idea into his first law.

[definition]
**Newton's first law (law of inertia):** a body stays at rest, or
keeps moving uniformly in a straight line, unless a net external
force acts on it.
[/definition]

**Inertia** is a body's resistance to any change of state, and
**mass** is its measure. Passengers lurch backward when a bus
starts and forward when it brakes — inertia at work.

[example]
In a collision the car stops but the passenger keeps moving
forward — inertia. The seatbelt supplies the backward force so
the passenger is not thrown onto the wheel.
[/example]

## 2. The Second Law: F = ma

@math label="eq:newton-second-law"
F = ma
@/math

The unit of force is the **newton**: 1 N accelerates a 1 kg mass at
1 m/s². The same force gives a heavier mass a smaller acceleration
— doubling the mass halves the acceleration.

[example]
**Worked example.** A 2 kg block accelerates at 5 m/s²:
F = 2 × 5 = 10 N. If the same 10 N pushes a 4 kg block,
a = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5 m/s².
[/example]

## 3. The Third Law: Action and Reaction

For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction — and the
two forces act on *different bodies*, so they never cancel.

A rocket pushes hot gas downward; the gas pushes the rocket upward.
A swimmer pushes water back; the water drives the swimmer forward.
A gun recoils as the bullet races ahead.

@diagram tree id="force-family" layout="tree" aria="Forces classified into contact and non-contact types"
  "Force" -> "Contact: friction, normal, tension, air drag"
  "Force" -> "Non-contact: gravitational, magnetic, electrostatic"
@/diagram

[warning]
Action and reaction act on two different bodies — that is exactly
why they never cancel each other's motion.
[/warning]

## 4. Momentum and Impulse

**Momentum** is the "quantity of motion" — it measures the impact a
moving body can deliver.

@math label="eq:momentum"
p = mv
@/math

The second law, read fully, says force is the rate of change of
momentum. **Impulse** = force × time = the momentum change it
produces.

[example]
A cricketer draws the hands back while catching a fast ball:
stretching the collision time shrinks the force. Airbags and
seatbelts work the same way; a karate chop keeps the time tiny, so
the force is huge.
[/example]

## 5. Conservation of Momentum

[definition]
In an isolated system the total momentum before an interaction
equals the total momentum after it.
[/definition]

[example]
**Collision.** A 2 kg trolley moving at 3 m/s couples with a
stationary 1 kg trolley. Total p = 6 kg·m/s, so the joined 3 kg
pair moves at 6 ÷ 3 = 2 m/s.
**Recoil.** A 3 kg gun fires a 0.02 kg bullet at 300 m/s
(p = 6 kg·m/s); the gun kicks backward at 6 ÷ 3 = 2 m/s.
[/example]

## 6. Friction

**Friction** opposes relative sliding between surfaces in contact;
it arises from microscopic interlocking of surface bumps.

| Type | Acts when | Relative size |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Static | no sliding yet | largest — a threshold |
| Kinetic | surfaces are sliding | smaller than static |
| Rolling | wheels or balls roll | smallest of the three |

Advantages: walking, braking, gripping, striking a match. Trouble:
wear, heat, and wasted energy — reduced with lubricants, polishing,
and ball bearings.

## 7. Circular Motion and Centripetal Force

A body moving in a circle is always accelerating toward the centre;
the inward net force is the **centripetal force**.

@math label="eq:centripetal-force"
F = \frac{mv^{2}}{r}
@/math

Mud flies off a spinning wheel *tangentially*: once it lets go, no
inward force remains and inertia carries it straight. Earth's
gravity supplies the centripetal force that keeps the Moon in orbit.

## 8. Quick Revision

| Concept | Formula / fact |
| --- | --- |
| First law | no net force → no change in motion |
| Second law | F = ma; 1 N = 1 kg·m/s² |
| Third law | equal, opposite, on different bodies |
| Momentum | p = mv (kg·m/s) |
| Impulse | force × time = change in momentum |
| Conservation | total p stays constant in an isolated system |
| Centripetal force | mv² ÷ r, directed to the centre |
